{{also|cajolé}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2015|August|4}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|cajoler}}, probably a blend of {{der|en|frm|cageoler||chatter like a jay}} (from {{m|frm|gajole}}, dialectal diminutive of {{m|frm|geai||jaybird}}) + {{der|en|fro|gaioler||entice into a cage}}, which is from {{der|en|ML.|gabiola}}, from {{der|en|LL.|caveola}}, diminutive of {{der|en|la|cavea||cage, coop, enclosure, stall}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/kəˈdʒəʊl/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|kə-jōlʹ}}, {{IPA|/kəˈdʒoʊl/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-cajole.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-au-cajole.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|əʊl|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|ca|jole|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|cajoles|cajoling|cajoled}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|and|intransitive}} To [[persuade]] someone to do something which they are [[reluctant]] to do, especially by [[flattery]] or [[promise]]s; to [[coax]].
#: {{syn|en|butter up|coax|entice|inveigle|sweet-talk|wheedle}}
#* '''1722''', [[w:Daniel Defoe|Daniel Defoe]], ''Moll Flanders'', ch. 12:
#*: Then he '''cajoled''' with his brother, and persuaded him what service he had done him.
#* '''1820''', [[w:Sir Walter Scott|Sir Walter Scott]], ''The Abbot'', ch. 27:
#*: If you are '''cajoled''' by the cunning arguments of a trumpeter of heresy, or the praises of a puritanic old woman, is not that womanish?
#* '''1894''', [[w:Horatio Alger|Horatio Alger]], ''Only An Irish Boy'', ch. 19:
#*: He had tried bullying, and without success. He would try '''cajoling''' and temptation.
#* '''1898''', [[w:Gilbert Parker|Gilbert Parker]], ''The Battle Of The Strong'', ch. 37:
#*: [W]ith eloquent arts he had '''cajoled''' a young girl into a secret marriage.
#* '''1917''', [[w:Upton Sinclair|Upton Sinclair]], ''King Coal'', ch. 8:
#*: Schulman, general manager of the "G. F. C.," had been sending out messengers to hunt for him, and finally had got him in his office, arguing and pleading, '''cajoling''' and denouncing him by turns.
#* '''2010''' August 4, Michael Scherer, "[http://www.time.com/time/politics/article/0,8599,2008429,00.html NonSTARTer? Obama's Troubled Nuclear Treaty]," ''Time'':
#*: For weeks, the White House, the Pentagon and Senate Democrats have been working overtime to '''cajole''', convince and placate Republicans.
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|date=2012-07-13|author=Alex Williams|title=Why Is It Hard to Make Friends Over 30?|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/15/fashion/the-challenge-of-making-friends-as-an-adult.html|passage= But the wife was visibly unimpressed by Ms. Baskin’s half-furnished home (they had just moved in) and thrown-together spaghetti dinner. “It was basically clear that his wife had been '''cajoled''' into attending,” said Ms. Baskin, 33. “She settled on to our rickety Ikea kitchen chairs like she was lowering herself into a coal mine.”}}

